course: dictionary
lesson: 4854
title: Hypnotise: asleep, or just fascinated?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To hypnotise someone means to put them into a deep, calm, dreamy state where they pay close attention. Read the clues around the word. In a story, a hypnotist swings a watch and speaks in a slow voice, so hypnotise is about calm, dreamy focus.
explain: The second meaning is about being fascinated. «The children were hypnotised by the flames» means they could not look away from the fire. Ask: what word would fit here? Try "fascinated" in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Be careful with cartoons. In films a snake hypnotises a rabbit with its stare, but snakes cannot hypnotise animals. They have no eyelids, so they seem to stare, and a snake charmer's cobra follows the moving pipe, not the music.
fact: Hypnotise is a verb: hypnotise, hypnotises, hypnotised, hypnotising.
fact: The nouns are hypnosis (the state) and hypnotist (the person who does it).
fact: In American spelling it is hypnotize.
fact: «Hypnotised by the flames» means so fascinated that you cannot look away.
fact: Real hypnosis, called hypnotherapy, is used by some therapists to help people relax; people under hypnosis are not asleep and cannot be made to do things against their will.
exercise: «The crowd was hypnotised by the bright silver ball.» What does hypnotised mean here? | Fast asleep | Angry and shouting | *So fascinated they could not look away | Bored and yawning
exercise: Ella says a snake can hypnotise a rabbit in cartoons. What is the true fact? | Snakes can hypnotise any animal they like | Snakes hypnotise rabbits by singing | Snakes hypnotise by flicking their tongues | *Snakes cannot hypnotise animals; their stare only looks spooky
exercise: Find and copy the noun that names the person who hypnotises. | *hypnotist | hypnotised | hypnosis | hypnotising
test: In a stage show, what does a hypnotist do? | *Puts people into a calm, dreamy state | Cooks the dinner for the audience | Runs very fast around the hall | Paints pictures on the stage
test: «Under hypnosis» a person is in a special state. What is true? | They always forget everything afterwards | They can be made to do anything at all | *They are not asleep and cannot be made to do things against their will | They are fast asleep
exam: Which word is the noun for the state of being hypnotised? | hypnotises | hypnotise | *hypnosis | hypnotising
